WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.417
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Welcome to "Get

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:03.570
Cooking in Cloud," where
we share the best recipes

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.520
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.690
I am Priyanka, and
in today's episode,

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:10.380
we'll share the recipe
to plan for data recovery

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.575
when production environment is
on premise or in another cloud.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.490
Main Street Style is
an e-commerce company

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.250
that has their production
environment set up on premise.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.140
Imagine if they were
to discover that they

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.940
have lost all the
recent customer

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.960
orders during a disaster.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.080
It would be a huge
loss for their business

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.240
if orders are not fulfilled.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.180
Since data is the critical
piece of their application,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.240
let's dive deeper into it
and help Main Street Style

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.110
with some strategies to avoid
losing data during a disaster.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:52.440
The term data covers two
scenarios, data backup

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.330
and database backups.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.420
Backing up data alone involves
copying a discrete amount

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.730
of data from one
place to another

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:03.120
to recover from corruption
or when production's down.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.170
Database backups,
on the other hand,

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.980
are slightly more complex,
because they typically involve

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.500
recovering to a point in time.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.080
Hence, we need to
not just consider

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.030
backing up the
database, but also

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.070
consider backing up
the transaction logs

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:21.090
and then applying them back to
the database during recovery.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.250
Now that we have the
basic understanding

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.040
of data and database
backups for DR,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.830
let's consider Main
Street Style's scenario,

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:32.790
and how they can set up
DR specifically for data.

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:36.280
For data backup and recovery,
there are a few options.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.550
They can create a
scheduled task that

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.280
runs a script or an
application to transfer

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.080
the data to cloud storage.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.540
Or, they can automate
a backup process

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.420
to cloud storage using
gsutil command line,

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.480
or by using one of the cloud
storage client libraries.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.280
The other option
for data recovery

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.210
is to use a third
party solution.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:59.280
Main Street Style uses the most
common tiered storage pattern

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.850
where they have the most recent
backups on faster storage,

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:06.990
and slowly migrate the older
backups to a cheaper storage.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.419
When they use Google
Cloud as their target,

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:12.210
they can use Cloud Storage
Nearline or Coldline

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.460
as the equivalents
of the slower tier.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.650
One way to do this
is to use a partner

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.920
gateway between on-premise
storage and cloud storage.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.840
Partner solutions
manage the transform

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.090
from an on-premise
network-attached storage

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.830
appliance or a
storage area network.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:29.920
Now that we've learned about
data storage and recovery,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.830
let's consider a database
backup and recovery.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.580
Main Street Style can use
a number of strategies

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.860
to implement a process
to recover a database

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.620
system from on-premise
to Google Cloud.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.380
Let's look at two of the
most common solutions.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.990
They could do a backup and
recovery using a recovery

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:49.740
server on Google Cloud, or
they could use a standby server

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.230
on Google Cloud for application.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.360
In the recovery
server approach, they

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.910
would create a
database backup using

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:01.270
the built-in backup mechanism of
the database management system.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:04.590
This will typically create
the backup to a local disk.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.410
Then, they would create
a Cloud Storage bucket

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.980
as a target for the data
backup in Google Cloud.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.530
Copy the backup files
to Google Cloud Storage

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:16.080
using gsutil or a
partner gateway solution

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.120
that we looked at earlier.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.400
Also, because this
is a database,

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.590
they would have to copy
the transaction logs

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.290
to the recovery site
on Google Cloud.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.630
Having a backup of
the transaction logs

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:30.240
helps keep the RPO values small.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.100
Then, they would
configure connectivity

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.340
to Google Cloud using
interconnect and VPN.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:38.130
Create a custom image
of the database server

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:42.000
on Google Cloud with exactly the
same configurations as the one

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.230
on-premise.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.800
Now, when time comes to recover
the database to the DR site

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.680
on Google Cloud, it's easy
for Main Street Style.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.200
They would start a
minimally-sized instance

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.970
from the custom
image, and attach

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:55.930
any persistent disks needed.

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.560
Set the autodelete
flag to no autodelete

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.250
so that the persistent disk will
not be inadvertently deleted,

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.470
since that could be a disaster.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.110
Then apply the latest
backup file and transaction

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.230
logs that we copied to
cloud storage, replace

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:13.050
the minimal instance with
the larger instance that's

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:15.570
capable of accepting
production traffic,

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:18.209
and finally, switch
the clients to point

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:20.660
to the recovered
database in Google Cloud.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:23.640
When a production environment
on-premise is up and running,

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.920
they would just have
to reverse the steps.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.830
Take a backup of the database
and transaction logs running

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:31.560
on Google Cloud, copy
these backup files

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.870
to the production
environment, and apply them

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.240
to the production
database system.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.490
Prevent clients from
connecting to the database

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.180
system in Google Cloud by
stopping the database service.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.910
From this point, the
application will be unavailable

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:48.040
until they finish restoring
the production environment.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.210
And finally, redirect the client
connections to the production

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.740
environment, and that's it.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.980
An alternative recipe is
to set up a standby server

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:00.030
on Google Cloud for application,
which helps achieve very

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:04.080
small RTO and RPO values
since it actually replicates

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:07.080
data and database
data in real time

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.110
to a hot standby of
the database server.

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:13.560
If Main Street Style was
just setup a standby server,

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.390
then here's how
they would do it.

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:18.420
They would first have to
connect their on-premise network

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.400
to the Google
Cloud network, then

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.870
create a custom image
of their database server

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:25.570
on Google Cloud in
the same configuration

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:27.250
as the one on-premise.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:29.080
Start an instance
from the custom image,

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:30.940
and attach any
persistent disks that

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:34.240
are needed with the
autodelete flag set to false.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.180
Then configure replication
between the on-premise database

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:40.750
server and the target database
server in Google Cloud.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:43.330
The clients are configured
in normal operation

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:45.790
to point to the database
server on-premise.

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:48.310
After configuring this
replication topology,

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:49.870
they would have
to switch clients

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:52.630
to point to the standby
server running in Google Cloud

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:53.950
network.

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:56.770
When production database
on-premise is up and running,

00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:59.710
they just resynchronize the
production database server

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.860
with the Google Cloud database
server and then switch clients

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:05.630
to point back to the
production environment.

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:07.720
Now, in our case,
Main Street Style

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:09.940
has production
setup on-premises.

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:12.520
But if they have the
production setup in AWS,

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:14.830
they can use the
storage transfer service

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:18.340
to transfer objects from
Amazon S3 to Cloud Storage.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:20.470
They could set up a
transfer job to schedule

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:24.070
periodic synchronizations
from data source to the sync,

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:27.700
with advanced filters based on
file creation date, file name

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:32.380
filters, and the times of the
day you prefer to import data.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:35.140
Or, they can also use
tools like Apache Airflow

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:37.610
to move data between clouds.

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:38.960
Well, there you have it.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.290
If you have a production
application on-premise

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:44.860
or in another cloud and need to
set up the Data Recovery, then

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:48.370
hopefully, you've learned
some strategies to do that.

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.590
That's all for today on
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:54.370
Here's hoping you're whipping
up your own DR strategy.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:57.190
Join us next time where
we will share more recipes

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:59.170
to implement DR for data.

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:01.250
If you would like to
see more such content,

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:04.440
don't forget to like and
subscribe to our channel.

